\section{Placement}{

Each call to \code{launch_worker()} selects the node whose share of launched
workers (\code{launched / cap}) is currently lowest, so workers are distributed
across nodes in proportion to their per-node \code{caps}. With a persistent worker
pool (\code{seconds_idle = Inf}, the \code{\link[=crew_controller_ssh]{crew_controller_ssh()}} default) and
\code{workers = sum(caps)}, each node receives exactly its capacity and no node is
over-committed. Placement is by cumulative launch count, so under worker churn
(crashes / relaunches) the proportions are preserved but per-node caps are not
strictly re-enforced; strict enforcement for transient pools is a planned
enhancement.
}

\section{Worker teardown}{

On normal shutdown the worker exits gracefully (the dispatcher closes the
connection, or \code{seconds_idle} / \code{seconds_wall} elapse), which also bounds how
long any stranded worker can survive. A \emph{hard} kill of the local \code{ssh} client
does not by itself guarantee the remote R exits; set \code{request_tty = TRUE} in
\code{\link[=crew_controller_ssh]{crew_controller_ssh()}} to launch with \code{ssh -tt} so the remote receives
\code{SIGHUP} and dies with the client (at the cost of merged/altered stdout-stderr
capture). Note that any \emph{child processes the worker spawns} (for example a
\verb{docker run} container) are owned by their own service and are not reaped by
SSH or \code{crew}; the worker code is responsible for tearing those down.
}

\section{Reverse-tunnel dial-back}{

With \code{tunnel = TRUE} (in \code{\link[=crew_controller_ssh]{crew_controller_ssh()}}) the dispatcher binds
\verb{127.0.0.1} and each worker is launched with \verb{ssh -R <node-port>:127.0.0.1:<dispatcher-port>},
so the worker dials a node-local port that is forwarded back to the dispatcher
over the existing SSH connection. No inbound firewall port is opened on the
control node, which suits locked-down networks. Each worker gets a distinct
node-local port (so co-located workers do not collide) and the tunnel lives
and dies with that worker's \code{ssh} process.
}

